<!--
    ServiceNow Update Set record-definitions for the SERVICE PORTAL PAGE
    LAYOUT of the CASE SUBMISSION page (`x_casemgmt_case_submit`) inside the
    scoped application `x_casemgmt`.

    WHY THIS FILE EXISTS (the defect it closes)
    ...........................................
    A Service Portal page renders nothing on its own. `sp_page` holds only
    page-level metadata (slug, title, anonymous-access flags); the content of
    a page is a chain of FOUR separate records:

        sp_page  <-  sp_container  <-  sp_row  <-  sp_column  <-  sp_instance
                                                                     |
                                                                     +-> sp_widget

    Neither this package nor the target instance carried a single row of that
    chain, so both public pages loaded (HTTP 200, no login wall) and painted
    a completely blank white viewport: 0 forms, 0 inputs, 0 buttons, and
    `GET /api/now/sp/page?id=x_casemgmt_case_submit` answering
    `containers: []`. The submission and lookup widgets were fully authored
    and healthy the whole time - they were simply orphaned, embedded nowhere.

    An earlier revision of `../pages/sp_page_x_casemgmt_case_submit.xml`
    attempted to express the layout as a JSON document in an element called
    `page_internal`. That element is NOT a field of `sp_page` on this release
    (its columns are category, css, draft, dynamic_title_structure,
    human_readable_url_structure, id, internal, omit_watcher, public, roles,
    seo_script, short_description, title, use_seo_script), so the platform
    discarded it silently on import. THIS file replaces that inert JSON with
    the four real records the renderer reads.

    WHAT IT AUTHORS (one record each, in dependency order)
    .....................................................
      1. sp_container  "Submit a Case - Container 1"  -> parents to the page
      2. sp_row        row 1 of that container
      3. sp_column     full-width (size 12) column of that row
      4. sp_instance   places ../widgets/sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_submission_widget.xml
                       in that column

    The single full-width column matches AAP Section 0.4.4's description of
    Page 1 - "a simple form with five inputs ... displays a confirmation
    panel" - which is one widget, full width, and nothing else. The widget
    itself renders its own <h2>Submit a Case</h2> heading and swaps in the
    confirmation widget after a successful POST, so the `sp_instance.title`
    is deliberately left EMPTY: setting it would paint a second, duplicate
    heading above the form.

    FIELD CHOICES (each copied from the shape the platform itself writes -
    read off the out-of-box `404` page's own layout chain on the target
    instance, so nothing here is invented):
      sp_container.width       = `container`  (the standard fixed-width
                                 Bootstrap container the OOB pages use)
      sp_container.class_name  = `m-t-lg`     (the OOB top-margin class, so
                                 the form is not flush against the header)
      sp_column.size           = 12           (full width of the 12-column
                                 Bootstrap grid)
      sp_instance.active       = true          (an inactive instance renders
                                 nothing - this is the flag that would
                                 silently re-blank the page)
      sp_instance.size         = md, color = default, order = 1  (OOB defaults)
      sp_instance.sys_class_name = sp_instance (sp_instance is an extendable
                                 table; the base class is what the renderer
                                 expects for a plain widget placement)

    REFERENCE RESOLUTION (AAP Section 0.5.2 dependency ordering)
    ...........................................................
    The four records reference each other, and the instance references the
    widget, by the package's own deterministic `sys_id` values - the same
    convention the package already uses for every other INTRA-PACKAGE
    metadata reference, for example `sp_portal.homepage` pointing at this
    page and `sys_ws_operation.web_service_definition` pointing at its
    scripted REST service. That is a deliberate distinction from the
    seed-data references (`case`, `organization`, `person`, `assigned_*`),
    which AAP Section 0.5.2 requires to resolve by human-readable key and
    which therefore carry no sys_id anywhere.

    It is also what makes the layout survive an Update Set preview on a
    fresh instance. Measured on the target PDI: the preview engine resolves
    a reference whose value is a sys_id when that sys_id is present EITHER
    in the target database OR as another canonically-named
    (`<table>_<sys_id>`) block of the same Update Set, and reports
    "Could not find a record in <table> for column <column> referenced in
    this update" otherwise - including for a value that is a display value
    or number, even when the target row exists. A seven-record probe of
    exactly this chain (a new widget plus container/row/column/instance
    referencing it) previewed with ZERO problems.

    THIS RECORD SET MUST LOAD AFTER:
      - ../../app/sys_app/x_casemgmt_case_management.xml   (the scope)
      - ../pages/sp_page_x_casemgmt_case_submit.xml        (the parent page)
      - ../widgets/sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_submission_widget.xml (the widget
        placed here, which in turn embeds
        ../widgets/sp_widget_x_casemgmt_case_confirmation_widget.xml at
        runtime through $sp.getWidget())

    VALIDATION-GATE MAPPING (AAP Section 0.7.3)
    ...........................................
      Gate 4 ("Portal - submission"): "Case created from unauthenticated
      portal submission - Case appears in internal list with Draft status
      and correct case number." Without these four records the gate can only
      ever pass at the REST layer; with them the user-facing page renders
      the five inputs, submits, and shows the returned case number.

    DETERMINISTIC IDENTIFIERS
    .........................
    Every `sys_id` below is the MD5 of its own `sys_update_name`, so
    re-generating this file is byte-identical and no random GUIDs appear:
      sp_container_x_casemgmt_case_submit             -> 062ae69ebc2fbbdddf116b1df0e78654
      sp_row_x_casemgmt_case_submit                   -> 585f95040e23064c31edb2f4200f8ee3
      sp_column_x_casemgmt_case_submit                -> 293bfde54e2e41d776858ac0c0b2623f
      sp_instance_x_casemgmt_case_submission_widget   -> 57db32ab0d3f77509dc1c0df98576e0b
    The referenced page (6a1f3c2ffd653613304512a734aab83b) and widget
    (b40f252d17be5fa3b3bb85cb79ca8d2f) sys_ids are the ones those two
    records already declare in this package.
-->
